>> title: Lime Pickle
>> category: Dressings & Dips
>> tags: indian, lime, pickle, chiles, condiment
>> counters: Curry House
>> aka: nimbu ka achar, lime achar, indian lime pickle, nimbu achar, hot lime pickle
>> pairs-with: papadom, raita
>> servings: 2 cups
>> time: 14 days
>> slack: unforgiving — the oil has to stand above the limes at all times; anything that breaks the surface goes mouldy and the jar is finished
>> step.1: salt and stand 7 days in the sun, shaken daily
>> step.2: toast 1 min, until they darken
>> step.3: grind coarse
>> step.4: fry in 1 min off the heat
>> step.5: stir together, jar, and stand 7 days more

Rest @limes{12}(quartered lengthwise, seeds picked out) under @kosher salt{1/3%cup}(80 g) and @ground turmeric{1%Tbs} in a clean #jar{} ~stand{7%days}, on a sunny windowsill, shaking it once a day until the skins have gone soft and translucent and the jar has filled with brine.

Toast @fenugreek seeds{1%Tbs} and @brown mustard seeds{2%Tbs} in a dry #skillet{} ~toast{1%min}, until they darken a shade — fenugreek goes bitter about four seconds after it goes brown, so pull the pan early.

Grind @&(~1)toasted seeds{} coarsely in a #spice grinder{}.

Fry @&(~1)ground seeds{}, @Kashmiri chile powder{3%Tbs} and @asafoetida{1/2%tsp} in @mustard oil{3/4%cup}(180 mL; heated to smoking, then taken off and cooled to warm) ~fry{1%min}, off the heat, in the residual warmth of the pan.

Stir @&(~4)cured limes and their brine{} into @&(~1)spiced oil{}, pack it all back into the jar so the oil covers everything, and ~stand{7%days} before opening.

Mustard oil is heated to smoking and cooled before use. The first week is the salt breaking down the pith.
